# Mazak VQC 20/40 main HAL loader
#
# WARNING:
# This is a skeleton. The hm2_7i80.* board name, pin names, board IP, Mesa firmware,
# resolver scales, drive polarity, normal states, and all safety-chain wiring are
# UNVERIFIED PLACEHOLDERS. Confirm the exact board tag (`hm2_7i80` expected) and the
# real generated HAL names from `readhmid` / `show pin hm2` before live testing.
#
# Selected architecture (2026-08-06 rev):
# LinuxCNC control PC connected to a Mesa 7i80HDT Ethernet FPGA host over hm2_eth
# (static IP). Daughter cards:
#   P1: 7i44 (RS-422 sserial breakout - one sserial port with 8 channels)
#        channel 0 -> 7i84U-A (existing 32/16 field I/O near green breakout PCB:
#                     ATC, hydraulic, coolant, air, utility, spindle digital I/O,
#                     hm2_7i80.0.7i84.0.0.*)
#        channel 1 -> 7i84U-B (limits, homes, drive-enables, probe, and five
#                     relay-driven loads: air/touch/tap blast, ATC barrier,
#                     flood, hm2_7i80.0.7i84.0.1.*)
#        channels 2-7 disabled (sserial_port_0="00xxxxxx"); FPGA pins released
#                     as spare GPIO for future MPG / 4th-axis / 7i84 expansion.
#   P2: 7i49 (3 resolver channels used out of 6 + 4 of 6 +/-10V analog outs used)
#   P3: unused/spare - all bare FPGA GPIO (no daughter card fitted). Probe was
#       previously wired here but was moved to 7i84U-B for 24V opto-isolation.
# Axis position feedback is RESOLVER via the 7i49 (5 kHz excitation baseline), reading
# the machine's original Tamagawa TS2014N resolvers - not quadrature encoders.
# All field I/O -- limits/homes/E-stop monitor/drive-enables/spindle digital/
# 100 VAC solenoids -- lives on the two 7i84U cards via sserial. The 7i80HDT
# directly owns only motion-critical signals: resolvers, +/-10V velocity
# commands, and the P3 probe input.

loadrt trivkins coordinates=XYZ
# num_dio=16 required by linuxcnc/atc_orient.hal + remap/toolchange.ngc, which use
# motion.digital-out-00..07 / digital-in-00..07 (motmod only creates 4 of each by default).
loadrt [EMCMOT]EMCMOT base_period_nsec=0 servo_period_nsec=[EMCMOT]SERVO_PERIOD num_joints=[KINS]JOINTS num_dio=16

# The 7i80HDT is an Ethernet board loaded via hm2_eth. Set board_ip to the confirmed
# static IP and adjust num_resolvers (7i49 on P2) / sserial (7i44 on P1) for the actual
# firmware. Axis feedback is resolver via the 7i49, so the firmware/config must expose
# resolver channels (num_resolvers) rather than plain encoders. Two 7i84Us appear on
# 7i44 -- port 0 = 7i84U-A, port 1 = 7i84U-B -- and both port strings must match the
# firmware.
# TODO: set the real board_ip and config string (incl. num_resolvers) from the 7i80HDT
# firmware / readhmid, and confirm the 7i49 pwmgen count as reported.
loadrt hostmot2
# num_pwmgens=4: the 7i49 +/-10V analog outputs ARE hostmot2 pwmgen instances (bipolar
# mode; there is no separate "analogout" object). Need >=4 for X/Y/Z + spindle;
# additional pwmgens will appear if the bitfile provisions more. Confirm the real
# count/instances against readhmid.
loadrt hm2_eth board_ip="192.168.1.121" config="num_encoders=1 num_resolvers=3 num_pwmgens=4 num_stepgens=0 sserial_port_0=00xxxxxx"
# board_ip: 192.168.1.121 (static, NIC enp0s31f6 at 192.168.1.1/24, confirmed pinging)
# num_resolvers=3: confirmed — three TS2014N shaft resolvers (X=00, Y=01, Z=02 ball screws)
#   Spindle does NOT use a resolver; it has a separate machine-side A/B/Z encoder.
# num_encoders=1: reserve one encoder channel for the spindle encoder (part# TBD).
#   Adjust count if readhmid shows a different layout for the encoder instances.
# num_pwmgens=4: 7i49 P2 AOUT0=X, AOUT1=Z, AOUT2=Y, AOUT3=spindle velocity.
#   AOUT4 reserved for FR-SX orient reference; AOUT5 spare. All bipolar +/-10V.
# sserial_port_0=00xxxxxx: enable channels 0 and 1 in mode 0 (00) and disable
#   channels 2..7 (xxxxxx). Per hostmot2(9), each character in the sserial_port_N
#   string configures ONE CHANNEL of a single sserial port (each port has 1-8
#   channels). Both 7i84Us live on port 0: channel 0 = 7i84U-A, channel 1 = 7i84U-B.
#   Disabling channels 2..7 with "x" releases those FPGA pins as spare GPIO instead
#   of tying them up as unused sserial. sserial_port_1..3 are NOT declared because
#   the 7i80HDT+7i44 firmware only instantiates one sserial port on P1.
#   Ref: https://linuxcnc.org/docs/html/man/man9/hostmot2.9.html (sserial_port_N).

loadrt pid names=pid.x,pid.y,pid.z,pid.s
loadrt estop-latch
# NOTE: do NOT loadrt threads — [EMCMOT]EMCMOT above creates the servo-thread from INI SERVO_PERIOD

addf hm2_7i80.0.read           servo-thread
addf motion-command-handler    servo-thread
addf motion-controller         servo-thread
addf pid.x.do-pid-calcs        servo-thread
addf pid.y.do-pid-calcs        servo-thread
addf pid.z.do-pid-calcs        servo-thread
addf pid.s.do-pid-calcs        servo-thread
addf estop-latch.0             servo-thread
addf hm2_7i80.0.write          servo-thread

# Axis PID gains are intentionally zero in the INI placeholders.
#
# The TRA drives close their own inner velocity loop on the motor tach
# (Tamagawa TGF-3D P402-Sx). LinuxCNC is the OUTER position loop driving those
# drives with an analog velocity reference via 7i49 pwmgen.NN.value. Commission
# the position loop with the FF1-first procedure in docs/servo_commissioning.md:
#   1. Confirm resolver direction/scale and analog output polarity.
#   2. Verify zero-command null at each 7i49 AOUT_N (|V_out| <= 50 mV).
#   3. Measure volts-per-speed with pid.output driven manually from halcmd at
#      low MAX_OUTPUT; set OUTPUT_SCALE per axis so pid.output units are in/s.
#   4. Set FF1 first (typically ~1.0 once OUTPUT_SCALE is calibrated).
#   5. Add P in small steps.
#   6. Add I only for residual bias, D only if end-of-move overshoot demands it.
#
# Do NOT tune P/I/D or FF1 before step 3 is complete on that axis.
# PID doc: 'When using FF1 tuning, scaling must be set so that output is in
# user units per second.'
# PID doc direction warning: 'If some output is in the wrong direction,
# negating gains to fix it is a mistake; set the scaling correctly elsewhere
# instead.'
setp pid.x.P                      [JOINT_0]P
setp pid.x.I                      [JOINT_0]I
setp pid.x.D                      [JOINT_0]D
setp pid.x.FF0                    [JOINT_0]FF0
setp pid.x.FF1                    [JOINT_0]FF1
setp pid.x.FF2                    [JOINT_0]FF2
setp pid.x.bias                   [JOINT_0]BIAS
setp pid.x.deadband               [JOINT_0]DEADBAND
setp pid.x.maxoutput              [JOINT_0]MAX_OUTPUT
# error-previous-target: use previous target in error calc — better servo behavior
setp pid.x.error-previous-target  true
# maxerror: clamp error before PID math — prevents windup on large following errors
setp pid.x.maxerror               [JOINT_0]MAX_ERROR

setp pid.y.P                      [JOINT_1]P
setp pid.y.I                      [JOINT_1]I
setp pid.y.D                      [JOINT_1]D
setp pid.y.FF0                    [JOINT_1]FF0
setp pid.y.FF1                    [JOINT_1]FF1
setp pid.y.FF2                    [JOINT_1]FF2
setp pid.y.bias                   [JOINT_1]BIAS
setp pid.y.deadband               [JOINT_1]DEADBAND
setp pid.y.maxoutput              [JOINT_1]MAX_OUTPUT
setp pid.y.error-previous-target  true
setp pid.y.maxerror               [JOINT_1]MAX_ERROR

setp pid.z.P                      [JOINT_2]P
setp pid.z.I                      [JOINT_2]I
setp pid.z.D                      [JOINT_2]D
setp pid.z.FF0                    [JOINT_2]FF0
setp pid.z.FF1                    [JOINT_2]FF1
setp pid.z.FF2                    [JOINT_2]FF2
setp pid.z.bias                   [JOINT_2]BIAS
setp pid.z.deadband               [JOINT_2]DEADBAND
setp pid.z.maxoutput              [JOINT_2]MAX_OUTPUT
setp pid.z.error-previous-target  true
setp pid.z.maxerror               [JOINT_2]MAX_ERROR

# Spindle PID — wired in motion_7i80hdt.hal once spindle feedback is confirmed.
# Gains are zero until spindle feedback (machine-side A/B/Z encoder via
# hm2_7i80.0.encoder.NN.velocity) is verified. Spindle does NOT use a 7i49
# resolver channel; num_resolvers=3 above allocates only X/Y/Z.
setp pid.s.P                      [SPINDLE_0]P
setp pid.s.I                      [SPINDLE_0]I
setp pid.s.D                      [SPINDLE_0]D
setp pid.s.FF0                    [SPINDLE_0]FF0
setp pid.s.FF1                    [SPINDLE_0]FF1
setp pid.s.FF2                    [SPINDLE_0]FF2
setp pid.s.bias                   [SPINDLE_0]BIAS
setp pid.s.deadband               [SPINDLE_0]DEADBAND
setp pid.s.maxoutput              [SPINDLE_0]MAX_OUTPUT
setp pid.s.error-previous-target  true
setp pid.s.maxerror               [SPINDLE_0]MAX_ERROR

#####################################################################
# Safety chain: E-stop latch + iocontrol enable
#####################################################################
#
# LinuxCNC's standard E-stop pattern using the estop-latch component
# (see estop_latch(9) man page for pin details):
#
#   estop-monitor (7i84U-A IN29)   -> estop-latch.0.ok-in
#     Physical E-stop chain aux contact: TRUE when NOT tripped. Any drop to
#     FALSE forces the latch to Faulted.
#   estop-reset                    -> estop-latch.0.reset
#     Momentary pulse that transitions the latch Faulted -> OK when ok-in and
#     fault-in permit. Sourced from iocontrol.0.user-request-enable so the
#     machine-on button clears the latch.
#   estop-latch.0.ok-out           -> iocontrol.0.emc-enable-in
#     Motion module enable. Also carried by 'estop-ok' for downstream readers
#     (atc_orient.hal, field_7i84u.hal).
#   estop-latch.0.fault-in         (unconnected - spare)
#     Wire drive-fault-any here (or any other summary-alarm bit) if desired
#     to force a fault beyond the physical E-stop chain.
#
# CAUTION - hardware chain remains authoritative:
#   The OEM MAR relay hardware E-stop chain is the primary safety. See
#   docs/estop_safety_chain.md for the required hardwired safety schematic
#   and fault-injection matrix that must be validated before drives are
#   energized under retrofit control. Per estop_latch(9), this component is
#   'part of a simple software ESTOP chain' - not the whole chain. This latch
#   is the SOFTWARE monitor of that chain (see wiring/front_control_panel_wiring.md).
#   estop-monitor comes from a dry-contact aux on the MAR relay via an
#   interposing relay; it does NOT bypass any hardware interlock.

net estop-reset       <= iocontrol.0.user-request-enable
net estop-reset       => estop-latch.0.reset
net estop-monitor     => estop-latch.0.ok-in
net estop-ok          <= estop-latch.0.ok-out
net estop-ok          => iocontrol.0.emc-enable-in

# estop-reset also feeds atc_orient.hal to clear its latched ATC / orient
# alarms in sync with the LinuxCNC re-enable. Additional readers pick up the
# same signal declared above.

# air-ok signal is sourced from 7i84U-A input-11 in field_7i84u.hal. If that
# terminal is not yet wired, uncomment the following to force TRUE for bench
# testing (comment it back out before energising anything):
# sets air-ok true

# spindle-enable (open-loop) is currently sourced by spindle.0.on via the
# spindle-ena net in field_7i84u.hal. atc_orient.hal reads spindle-enable
# separately; alias it here so the ATC component sees the same signal.
# (Both nets carry the same value; alias saves declaring a second writer.)
net spindle-enable    <= spindle.0.on
